dabout way of saying  that if you actually want to compare if  two things are actually  equal you use the identical function and  this is one of those  being a user of r versus being a  developer of r  you have to use more robust uh functions  when you are  trying to develop packages  uh we wrote about this essentially the  takeaway is  uh the way quote works if a expression  if what  the way quote works is it will take an  expression  and if it's longer than 60 characters it  will create a vector  60 character in 60 character chunks but  when you use double equals on an  expression and only compares the first  value so that's why it comes out to be  true and  the everything at the end will be  ignored  so learn r versus great this if you're  trying to work with this just this is  the one thing to keep in mind  learn r is is responsible for expression  capturing  uh data reporting uh if you need to  capture how many correct answers a  student got  how much time all of that stuff is  captured in the learn r system  and you essentially have to use that  system to build out  uh like something that you would use for  like a mooc for example or to grade your  own classwork  great this is just responsible for  checking the solution  and reporting back some kind of  statement  whether whether that is just a simple  you got this right or wrong or actually  that  difficult statement of where you got  your  answer wrong  so learn r is actually a very  complicated piece of software  um you essentially need to know all  about how knitter works our markdown  works and how shiny works  it is essentially a big capstone project  one of the current  our studio interns this summer um  nistral  he actually wrote a blog post on  learning how they learn our system works  so if you want to learn more about  all of these tools uh that's a pretty  good blog post to start and he's  definitely  i spent most of my summer just  understanding how uh capturing  expressions work  and so he's actually learning about this  this insane project  all of the exercise components are  passed into the exercise checker  so this exercise checker we in  grade this provide a grade learn our  function but if you  need to do something else this is where  you would put in your own gradar  function or grading function  and everything from learn r like those  parts in the code chunks that all gets  passed  passed into this exercise checker  function so your exercise tracker  function needs to be able to take in  those components  and then you can do whatever you want  with it  how do we deal with pipes so in grade  this we actually go through the  process of unpiping any code that is  piped  so if you do something like df pipe into  dr select column one  the first thing that we do is we detect  if you use pipes and then unpipe all the  code before we do the ast checking  so that's that's why some of these  statements  seem to be um a little  wonky uh when you read them so uh  one other thing you can do is you can  actually use grade this as a standalone  code checker  so for example we can library the grade  this library  let's say the student passed in the  value of five we can actually call grade  result directly  and say if they got the value of five  pass  and here's the magic sauce is that there  is actually an argument called learn our  args  it takes a value called last value and  then this is what it's being used  internally to check  uh the student result  and then you get a a graded object back  a greater graded object back which is  really just a list  uh with a message and whether or not the  answer was correct  you can also use this to gray code so we  can quote uh arbitrary expressions i'm  going to set it to student code and  solution code  and then you can also call the graycode  function directly  and then the magic sauce here is there  is a greater arts parameter which takes  a list  and takes in those two expressions and  then you get the same greater graded  object back which is just a message and  whether or not it is correct  one of the things i learned during the  internship is actually how tidy eval  works so in about two minutes i'm going  to explain to you how this works  uh we've seen formulas in ggplot and  writing our  formulas for models and we can say tilde  x  carat two and we can actually save this  to a variable  it is a formula and has that expression  and what we can actually do is take the  second argument  of formula and it gives in the actual  quoted expression  and that's sort of that's and we can do  that with anything with the tilde and  that's how the tilde  works um and then from tidy eval we can  say  what is the expression so this x carat  two and the magic sauce here  is we can pass in a data parameter  defining  what all of those variables in the  expression mean so we can say  in that expression we're going to set x  equals to five  if we want we can also provide a an  environment if it's not provided in data  and then when we eval this expression  it calculates it and so in actual  practice  that little bit of code is actually what  we use and grade this  and so what i just showed you is exactly  that same mechanic  um and so this is an art conference but  uh nistral is actually working on the  python port upgrade this  and so you will be able to do code  checking in python  uh right now you can actually still use  the uh result checking in python because  of reticulate and how articulate works  but we are slowly working on uh  implementing this from the python world  and so with that i would say thank you  for my talk uh coming to this talk and  you can find me here  and uh the slides are there and right  after this i will be posting the slides  as the pdf  so thanks thank you dan  so now we come to our lunch break we are  miraculously  ahead of schedule this has never  happened in the past five years  of the seven different conferences we've  run we've never been ahead of schedule  we